**What changed:** In Angular 20.1.0, the Angular template expression syntax and compiler support for new binary assignment operators in templates, including `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, `%=`, `**=`, `&&=`, `||=`, and `??=` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62064, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0dcf230d52694e6d3d6e55d6e675d55f9cf236bc). The compiler's AST visitor interface `AstVisitor` and the `Binary` class (in `@angular/compiler`) now handle these operations. In 20.0.0, the `Binary` class was present but did not support these new assignment operators in templates; the `Binary.isAssignmentOperation` method did not include these operations (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/compiler/src/expression_parser/ast.ts). In 20.1.0, `Binary.isAssignmentOperation` explicitly supports these operators, and the `Chain` class expressions type changed from `any[]` to `AST[]` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.1.0/packages/compiler/src/expression_parser/ast.ts). The `BindingPipe` class also changed its `type` field to an optional `type` enum of `BindingPipeType`, and the `errors` type in `ASTWithSource` changed from `ParserError[]` to `ParseError[]` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.1.0/packages/compiler/src/expression_parser/ast.ts).
**Affects:** Applications using Angular 20.0.0 and upgrading to 20.1.0, specifically those using template expressions with binary assignment operators or relying on internal compiler AST types (`Binary`, `AstVisitor`, `Chain`, `BindingPipe`, `ASTWithSource`). Applications using new template features like `+=` or `??=` in templates.
**Action:** required: re-verify that custom template expressions using `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, `%=`, `**=`, `&&=`, `||=`, `??=` compile correctly, ensuring the `Binary` class handles them. Applications using `Chain`, `BindingPipe`, `ASTWithSource`, or `AstVisitor` should review the type changes and add the new optional `visitUnary` method to custom AST visitors if necessary, and update type definitions to match the new `type` field in `BindingPipe`.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// In 20.1.0 (packages/compiler/src/expression_parser/ast.ts)
export class Binary extends AST {
    constructor(
      span: ParseSpan,
      sourceSpan: AbsoluteSourceSpan,
      public operation: string,
      public left: AST,
      public right: AST,
    ) {
      super(span, sourceSpan);
    }
    override visit(visitor: AstVisitor, context: any = null): any {
      return visitor.visitBinary(this, context);
    }

    static isAssignmentOperation(op: string): boolean {
      return (
        op === '=' ||
        op === '+=' ||
        op === '-=' ||
        op === '*=' ||
        op === '/=' ||
        op === '%=' ||
        op === '**=' ||
        op === '&&=' ||
        op === '||=' ||
        op === '??='
      );
    }
  }
```

**What changed:** The Angular compiler's `Parser` class (in `packages/compiler/src/expression_parser/parser.ts`) previously held a single instance-level `errors: ParserError[]` array that was shared and propagated across all expression ASTs parsed from a template. Consequently, if one binding contained an invalid expression syntax, the resulting `ParserError` entries were duplicated and incorrectly propagated to other valid AST nodes in the same template ((https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61793), (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/5a76826d266b4ed0ef863221571e4b6b1b16182f)).
In Angular 20.1.0, the `Parser` instance-level `errors` array was removed. Instead, internal methods such as `parseAction`, `parseBinding`, `parseSimpleBinding`, `parseTemplateBindings`, `parseInterpolation`, `splitInterpolation`, and `_checkNoInterpolation` now allocate and pass a unique, local `errors: ParserError[]` array per parsed expression AST, ensuring parser errors are only reported on the specific node where the syntax error occurred. (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/5a76826d266b4ed0ef863221571e4b6b1b16182f.patch)
**Affects:** Applications or tooling that directly utilize the internal `@angular/compiler` `Parser` API, specifically those implementing or extending `Parser` or invoking internal methods like `splitInterpolation` whose signatures have been updated to include an explicit `errors` parameter. Standard application templates are unaffected by build-breaking changes but will see improved, de-duplicated error reporting.
**Action:** required: Update any direct, non-public usage of the `@angular/compiler` `Parser` class's internal methods (such as `splitInterpolation`) to include the new `errors` parameter, and re-verify template error message expectations in test suites.

**Before/after:**
```diff
-  private errors: ParserError[] = [];
-
-  constructor(
-    private _lexer: Lexer,
-    private _supportsDirectPipeReferences = false,
-  ) {}
+  constructor(
+    private readonly _lexer: Lexer,
+    private readonly _supportsDirectPipeReferences = false,
+  ) {}

-    this._checkNoInterpolation(input, location, interpolationConfig);
-    // ... passed this.errors to parser
-    return new ASTWithSource(ast, input, location, absoluteOffset, this.errors);
+    const errors: ParserError[] = [];
+    this._checkNoInterpolation(errors, input, location, interpolationConfig);
+    // ... passed errors to parser
+    return new ASTWithSource(ast, input, location, absoluteOffset, errors);
```

**What changed:** Previously, when the template parser hit an error inside interpolated text (e.g. `{{foo[0}}` mixed into a long text node), the compiler reported a `Parser Error` spanning the entire surrounding text node, producing noisy diagnostics in long strings. In 20.1.0 the `Parser` in `packages/compiler/src/expression_parser/parser.ts` uses the source span of the individual interpolated expression token (`interpolatedTokens?.[i * 2 + 1]?.sourceSpan`) when parsing each interpolation, so errors are reported on the exact expression, e.g. the error span is now `{{foo[0}}` instead of the whole text node (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/089ad0ee15d6be9b2493bb67519cb59e0454a1ef, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62258). The error message text also changed: the bracketed expression no longer includes the `{{ }}` delimiters and column numbers are relative to the expression, e.g. `Parser Error: Bindings cannot contain assignments at column 5 in [ {{x = 2}}]` became `Parser Error: Bindings cannot contain assignments at column 5 in [x = 2]` (same commit, updated tests in `ngtsc_spec.ts` and `diagnostic_spec.ts`). This also affects Angular Language Service diagnostics (`getSemanticDiagnostics`).

**Affects:** Applications whose templates contain interpolation expressions with parser errors (invalid syntax) and that assert on, snapshot, or otherwise match the exact text/spans of compiler or Language Service `Parser Error` diagnostics.

**Action:** none — code compiles as before; only the location and message format of existing template parser error diagnostics change. If tooling, tests, or CI log-parsing match on Parser Error message strings (e.g. `in [{{expr}}]`) or error spans, re-verify that matching still works.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (Angular 20.0) — error spans whole text node, message includes {{ }}:
// 'Parser Error: Bindings cannot contain assignments at column 8 in [{{nope = true}}]'

// After (Angular 20.1) — error spans only the expression, message drops {{ }}:
// 'Parser Error: Bindings cannot contain assignments at column 8 in [nope = true]'
// Error spans from r3_template_transform_spec: '{{foo[0}}', '{{.bar}}', '{{one + #two + baz}}'
```

**What changed:** The `@angular/compiler` package removed its direct dependency on `typescript` within the linker implementation. Previously, the linker's reliance on TypeScript barrel files caused a 500ms to 1s compilation overhead by preventing proper tree-shaking and code splitting. TypeScript is no longer listed in the `dependencies` field of the compiler's `package.json` (only `tslib` remains), and the PR description states "TypeScript has now been changed to an optional peer dependency" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61618, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e9fcbb8af12e7b4370d2e03e6004f3f2fe02c981, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.1.0/packages/compiler/package.json).

**Affects:** Applications that depend on `@angular/compiler` for JIT compilation or linker processing and previously relied on `@angular/compiler` to transitively install `typescript`.

**Action:** required: re-verify that `typescript` is explicitly installed as a dependency in your project, as it is no longer automatically installed by `@angular/compiler`.

**Before/after:**
```json
// @angular/compiler/package.json (20.0)
// dependencies included "typescript"

// @angular/compiler/package.json (20.1)
{
  "dependencies": {
    "tslib": "^2.3.0"
  }
}
```

**What changed:** `@angular/compiler-cli` 20.1.0 adds experimental declaration-only emission (`.d.ts` extraction without full type-checking) that lets type-declaration emission be decoupled from full compilation, enabling parallel build phases. It activates only when both the TS `emitDeclarationOnly` option and the new internal `BazelAndG3Options._geminiAllowEmitDeclarationOnly?: boolean` flag are set; when on, the compiler forces `CompilationMode.LOCAL` ("we add experimental support for a declaration-only emission mode based on the local compilation mode"). In this mode the compiler cannot resolve external references or type information, so unsupported constructs now throw `FatalDiagnosticError`, e.g. "@Input decorators with a transform function are not supported in experimental declaration-only emission mode" (suggesting conversion to input signals) and "External references in host directives are not supported in experimental declaration-only emission mode". Previously compilation simply failed with `emitDeclarationOnly` and `noCheck` set. (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61334, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e62fb359d6da8e0458b18f24e6bff60602f93fc6)

**Affects:** Only projects that set the TS `emitDeclarationOnly` option together with the internal `_geminiAllowEmitDeclarationOnly` flag; not affected unless both are enabled. Described in the source as "specifically tailored to support fast type declaration emission for the Gemini app in g3".

**Action:** required: re-verify builds that enable `emitDeclarationOnly` plus `_geminiAllowEmitDeclarationOnly`; the fetched pages describe no migration step for applications not using these experimental options, and the feature is marked experimental and internal (no public API documented for ordinary apps).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// @public interface BazelAndG3Options (goldens/public-api/compiler-cli/compiler_options.api.md)
export interface BazelAndG3Options {
    annotateForClosureCompiler?: boolean;
    _geminiAllowEmitDeclarationOnly?: boolean; // added in 20.1.0
    generateDeepReexports?: boolean;
    generateExtraImportsInLocalMode?: boolean;
    onlyExplicitDeferDependencyImports?: boolean;
}

// compiler.ts activation
this.emitDeclarationOnly =
  !!options.emitDeclarationOnly && !!options._geminiAllowEmitDeclarationOnly;
```

**What changed:** This change adds support for direct external references in host directives when the experimental fast type declaration emission is used, introduced in Angular 20.0.0 (commit e62fb35) and enhanced in Angular 20.1.0 (commit 0cf1001). Previously, in the initial implementation of experimental fast type declaration emission, external references in host directives were not supported at all. The new behavior allows direct external references, though any other expressions that indirectly use external references remain unsupported (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0cf1001715d2f528c61735108d12e29047907d98, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61469).

**Affects:** Applications that have experimental fast type declaration emission enabled and use directives with host directives that contain direct external references.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (20.0): External references in host directives with experimental fast type declaration emission would fail or not be resolved.
// After (20.1): Direct external references in host directives are supported when experimental fast type declaration emission is enabled.
@Directive({
  host: {
    '[style]': 'externalReference.expression' // Supported as a direct external reference if enabled
  }
})
```

**What changed:** The Angular compiler-cli extended diagnostic `interpolatedSignalNotInvoked` (code `NG8109`) now also detects signal references that appear negated with the `!` operator in templates, instead of only flagging plain `PropertyRead` expressions. The check `InterpolatedSignalCheck` now unwraps a `PrefixNot` AST node before testing for a `PropertyRead`, so both interpolation `{{ !mySignal3 }}` and bound property `[prop]="!negatedSignal"` cases produce a warning when the signal is not invoked with `()` (<https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59970>, <https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/59970.diff>). Before this fix, only the first interpolation `{{ test }}` was reported and `{{ !test }}` was missed; now `!`-negated signals are also marked. The diagnostic only emits when `strictTemplates` is enabled in `angularCompilerOptions` (<https://angular.dev/extended-diagnostics>).

**Affects:** Projects with `strictTemplates: true` that use Angular signals and reference them in templates via negation (`{{ !someSignal() }}` / `[attr]="!someSignal"`) without calling the signal, and have `extendedDiagnostics.checks.interpolatedSignalNotInvoked` set to `error` (or `defaultCategory: "error"`).

**Action:** required: re-verify the affected templates; any signal that was only used negated and left uninvoked (e.g. `[value]="!isOpen"`) now produces a new `NG8109` warning. If such templates are legitimate or you want to preserve build success after promoting extended diagnostics to errors, demote the check via `angularCompilerOptions.extendedDiagnostics.checks.interpolatedSignalNotInvoked` to `"warning"` (or `"suppress"`) instead of letting it remain an error.
</thinking>

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Component
export class AppComponent {
  readonly test = signal(false);
}
```
```html
<!-- Template -->
{{ test }}
{{ !test }}
```
```json
{
  "angularCompilerOptions": {
    "strictTemplates": true,
    "extendedDiagnostics": {
      "checks": {
        "interpolatedSignalNotInvoked": "error"
      },
      "defaultCategory": "error"
    }
  }
}
```

**What changed:** The Angular compiler-cli option that enables fast type declaration emission (`emitDeclarationOnly` + `noCheck` mode, added experimentally in Angular 20.0 via PR #61334) was renamed from `_geminiAllowEmitDeclarationOnly` to `_experimentalAllowEmitDeclarationOnly` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/77fa204ad16fef561c009fcef0ab1fb92a37f986, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61353). The flag lives on the `BazelAndG3Options` interface in `@angular/compiler-cli` and is set under `angularCompilerOptions` in `tsconfig.json`; `NgCompiler` now computes `this.emitDeclarationOnly = !!options.emitDeclarationOnly && !!options._experimentalAllowEmitDeclarationOnly;` (patch). Not marked as a breaking change since the option was internal/experimental (PR #61353). The mode "extracts the type declarations (.d.ts) files without full type-checking" and is "specifically tailored to support fast type declaration emission for the Gemini app in g3" (patch, PR #61334).

**Affects:** Only applications/libraries that explicitly set `_geminiAllowEmitDeclarationOnly: true` in `angularCompilerOptions` (an experimental Bazel/G3-oriented flag); ordinary applications are unaffected.

**Action:** required: if `tsconfig.json` sets `_geminiAllowEmitDeclarationOnly: true` in `angularCompilerOptions`, rename the key to `_experimentalAllowEmitDeclarationOnly`; otherwise re-verify nothing — no change needed.

**Before/after:**
```jsonc
// tsconfig.json → angularCompilerOptions (before)
{ "_geminiAllowEmitDeclarationOnly": true }
// after
{ "_experimentalAllowEmitDeclarationOnly": true }
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.1.0 adds a `destroyed` boolean property to the `DestroyRef` API. The `onDestroy` method of `DestroyRef` previously threw an error if the `DestroyRef` instance was already destroyed. The new `destroyed` property allows developers to check if an instance (component, directive, or injector) has already been destroyed before attempting to register a cleanup callback, thus avoiding the exception (<https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61849>, <https://angular.dev/api/core/DestroyRef>, <https://angular.love/angular-20-1-whats-new>).
**Affects:** Applications that use `DestroyRef.onDestroy` in contexts where the `DestroyRef` might already be destroyed, such as within asynchronous callbacks or race conditions.
**Action:** none (optional: Developers can use `if (!this.destroyRef.destroyed) { ... }` to guard calls to `DestroyRef.onDestroy` to prevent exceptions if they encounter this specific race condition).
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before
this.destroyRef.onDestroy(() => { ... });

// After
if (!this.destroyRef.destroyed) {
  this.destroyRef.onDestroy(() => { ... });
}
```

**What changed:** `EnvironmentInjector` (previously only had `get`, `runInContext`, `destroy`, plus an `@internal` `destroyed` getter and `@internal` `onDestroy`) gained a public API surface in 20.1.0: `abstract get destroyed(): boolean` — "Indicates whether the instance has already been destroyed" — documented alongside the previously `@internal` `abstract onDestroy(callback: () => void): () => void`, mirroring `DestroyRef` (PR diff promotes `destroyed` from `/** @internal */` to public and adds it to the `EnvironmentInjector` golden in `goldens/public-api/core/index.api.md`). The `R3Injector` implementation sets `_destroyed = true` at the start of `destroy()`, before running `ngOnDestroy` hooks and `_onDestroyHooks`, which is why the doc does not mention whether callbacks have executed. `onDestroy` calls `assertNotDestroyed(this)`, so it throws if `destroyed` is already `true`, and returns an unregister function. The `DestroyRef.destroyed` doc was simplified from "destroyed and whether its `onDestroy` callbacks have executed" to just "already been destroyed". (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61951, https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/61951.diff, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/737b35b684663bb641939f05ae12fa11b3395159/packages/core/src/di/r3_injector.ts)

**Affects:** Applications that dynamically create injectors (e.g. via `createEnvironmentInjector`, `createNgModule`, `NgModuleRef.injector`, or `TestBed`) and previously checked `destroyed` via a cast to any / internal symbols, or that register cleanup on injectors. Purely additive API — no existing behaviour changes.

**Action:** none (opt-in capability: check `EnvironmentInjector.destroy` before re-creating an injector, and register cleanup with `EnvironmentInjector.onDestroy(callback)` instead of `DestroyRef.onDestroy` when the scope is an injector; note `onDestroy` throws once `destroyed === true`).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/core/src/di/r3_injector.ts — EnvironmentInjector (Angular 20.1.0)
abstract destroy(): void;

/**
 * Indicates whether the instance has already been destroyed.
 */
abstract get destroyed(): boolean;

/**
 * @internal
 */
abstract onDestroy(callback: () => void): () => void;
```

**What changed:** `TestBed.createComponent` gained an optional second argument (`TestComponentOptions`) accepting a `bindings?: Binding[]` array, so test components can be created with inputs/outputs bound directly instead of via a wrapper/test-host component or post-creation `ComponentRef.setInput()` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62040). The `Binding` helpers `inputBinding`, `outputBinding` and `twoWayBinding` exported from `@angular/core` (added for dynamically-created components in v20.0) are used here, e.g. `TestBed.createComponent(User, { bindings: [inputBinding('userModel', userModel), outputBinding('selected', handler)] })`. `inputBinding` takes the input name plus a signal or a function returning the value; `outputBinding` takes the output name plus a callback (ideal for spies); `twoWayBinding` requires a writable signal. It works with `signal()`/`input()` APIs and with `@Input`/`@Output` decorators, honors input aliases (the alias name must be used), updates propagate when the signal changes after `fixture.detectChanges()`, a missing required input throws at initialization, and a non-existent property name throws at runtime (property names are strings, so there is no type checking) (https://timdeschryver.dev/blog/using-the-new-bindings-api-to-test-angular-components-with-angular-testing-library).

**Affects:** Angular unit tests using `TestBed.createComponent` for components with inputs/outputs (including the removed wrapper/test-host pattern and `fixture.componentRef.setInput(...)` setups). Purely additive API; no existing test code breaks.

**Action:** none — existing tests keep working. Optionally re-verify new tests that adopt `bindings`; note `TestBed.createComponent` still does not trigger change detection synchronously, so `fixture.detectChanges()` (or `TestBed.tick()`) is needed for bindings to apply.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before: set inputs after creation (or use a wrapper component)
const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(QuizStatusComponent);
fixture.componentRef.setInput('timeLeft', 10);

// After (Angular 20.1): bind at creation time
TestBed.configureTestingModule({}).createComponent(QuizStatusComponent, {
  bindings: [
    inputBinding('timeLeft', () => 10),
    inputBinding('status', () => ({ correct: 0, incorrect: 0 })),
  ],
});
```
(Snippet from  and Medium Ng-News search result, corroborated by the official guide https://angular.dev/guide/testing/components-scenarios which shows `inputBinding('value', value)` / `outputBinding('valueChange', ...)` inside `bindings: [...]`.)

Note: Angular Testing Library v18.1.0 mirrors this via a `bindings` option on `render`, deprecating `componentProperties`/`inputs`/`on`.
