**What changed:** `TestBed.createComponent` gained an optional second argument (`TestComponentOptions`) with a `bindings?: Binding[]` property, so test components can be created with inputs/outputs bound directly instead of via a wrapper/test-host component or post-creation `ComponentRef.setInput()` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62040). The `Binding` helpers `inputBinding`, `outputBinding` and `twoWayBinding` exported from `@angular/core` are used here, e.g. `TestBed.createComponent(User, { bindings: [inputBinding('userModel', userModel), outputBinding('selected', handler)] })`. `inputBinding` takes the input name plus a signal or a function returning the value; `outputBinding` takes the output name plus a callback (useful as a spy); `twoWayBinding` requires a writable signal. It works with signal-based `input()`/`output()` and with `@Input`/`@Output` decorators, honors input aliases (the alias name must be used), propagates later signal changes after `fixture.detectChanges()`, throws if a required input is missing, and throws at runtime for a non-existent property name (names are plain strings, so there is no type checking) (https://timdeschryver.dev/blog/using-the-new-bindings-api-to-test-angular-components-with-angular-testing-library).

**Affects:** Angular unit tests that use `TestBed.createComponent` on components with inputs/outputs, including tests using wrapper/test-host components or `fixture.componentRef.setInput(...)`. Purely additive API — no existing test code breaks.

**Action:** none — existing tests keep working. When adopting the new API, re-verify that bindings are applied via change detection, since `TestBed.createComponent` does not trigger change detection synchronously (call `fixture.detectChanges()` or use `TestBed.tick()`).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before: set inputs after creation
const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(QuizStatusComponent);
fixture.componentRef.setInput('timeLeft', 10);

// After (Angular 20.1)
TestBed.configureTestingModule({}).createComponent(QuizStatusComponent, {
  bindings: [
    inputBinding('timeLeft', () => 10),
    inputBinding('status', () => ({ correct: 0, incorrect: 0 })),
  ],
});
```
(Snippet from the 20.1 release write-ups; corroborated by the official guide https://angular.dev/guide/testing/components-scenarios showing `inputBinding('value', value)` / `outputBinding('valueChange', ...)` inside `bindings: [...]`.)

**What changed:** In `@angular/core/testing`'s `fake_async.ts`, the `fakeAsyncTest` zone module (`_Zone[_Zone.__symbol__('fakeAsyncTest')]`) was previously resolved once at module-load time into a constant, so `fakeAsync`, `tick`, `flush`, `flushMicrotasks`, `discardPeriodicTasks`, and `resetFakeAsyncZone` failed if the `zone-testing` polyfill was loaded *after* `@angular/core/testing`. The fix (PR #61375, commit 4356e85456, in 20.1.0) resolves the module lazily inside a `getFakeAsyncTestModule()` / `withFakeAsyncTestModule()` helper at each call, so `fakeAsync` works regardless of whether the zone-testing polyfill is imported before or after `@angular/core/testing`. The error message when zone-testing is missing at call time is unchanged: "zone-testing.js is needed for the fakeAsync() test helper but could not be found. Please make sure that your environment includes zone.js/testing" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61375/files, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/4356e8545666f13033bb2c4b7fa018d0b97e6f01).

**Affects:** Tests using `fakeAsync`/`tick`/`flush`/`flushMicrotasks`/`discardPeriodicTasks`/`resetFakeAsyncZone` from `@angular/core/testing` where `zone.js/testing` (zone-testing polyfill) is set up after `@angular/core/testing` is loaded — previously these helpers threw even though zone-testing was eventually loaded.

**Action:** none — this is a bug fix; no code, config, or dependency change is required. Applications that previously reordered imports (or worked around the load-order failure) can re-verify that `fakeAsync` tests pass without such workarounds.

**Before/after:**
```diff
 const _Zone: any = typeof Zone !== 'undefined' ? Zone : null;
-const fakeAsyncTestModule = _Zone && _Zone[_Zone.__symbol__('fakeAsyncTest')];
+function getFakeAsyncTestModule() {
+  return _Zone && _Zone[_Zone.__symbol__('fakeAsyncTest')];
+}

-export function fakeAsync(fn: Function, options?: {flush?: boolean}): (...args: any[]) => any {
-  if (fakeAsyncTestModule) {
-    return fakeAsyncTestModule.fakeAsync(fn, options);
-  }
-  throw new Error(fakeAsyncTestModuleNotLoadedErrorMessage);
+export function fakeAsync(fn: Function, options?: {flush?: boolean}): (...args: any[]) => any {
+  return withFakeAsyncTestModule((v) => v.fakeAsync(fn, options));
 }
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/core`'s hydration code, `serializeLView` in `packages/core/src/hydration/annotate.ts` now records the template `ssrId` into `ngh[TEMPLATES][noOffsetIndex]` for any `isLContainer(lView[i])` with a `tNode.tView`, *before* the disconnected-node check, instead of only inside the later non-excluded branch. Previously "projected nodes were missing ssrId information and were skipping annotating template information, which caused templates to be destroyed and recreated rather than hydrated" (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8424b3bcd5b9c78f37dc8ba636c87775937bcc03). `locateOrCreateContainerAnchorImpl` in `packages/core/src/render3/instructions/template.ts` now reads `const ssrId = lView[HYDRATION]?.data[TEMPLATES]?.[index] ?? null;` and applies it to the underlying `TView` *before* the `isNodeCreationMode` early return to `createContainerAnchorImpl`, so creation-mode templates get the server `ssrId`. Fixes issue #50543: with hydration enabled and a parent-supplied `ng-template` rendered via `NgTemplateOutlet` (e.g. `@ContentChild('customTemplate', {static: true})`), content was rendered twice during hydration (`abc abc` then `abc`) because the embedded view was destroyed and recreated (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/50543). The PR is marked "No" breaking change (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61989).

**Affects:** SSR apps with hydration enabled (`provideClientHydration`) that render a projected/`ContentChild` `TemplateRef` through `<ng-container [ngTemplateOutlet]="...">` or other content-projected containers.

**Action:** none — no code, config or dependency change is required; re-verify that such template outlets no longer flash/double-render during hydration.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// packages/core/src/render3/instructions/template.ts
-  // Regular creation mode.
-  if (isNodeCreationMode) {
-    return createContainerAnchorImpl(tView, lView, tNode, index);
-  }
-
-  const hydrationInfo = lView[HYDRATION]!;
-  const ssrId = hydrationInfo.data[TEMPLATES]?.[index] ?? null;
+  const ssrId = lView[HYDRATION]?.data[TEMPLATES]?.[index] ?? null;
```

