```

**What changed:** Angular 20.1.0 adds support for the Fetch API's `cache` and `priority` options in `httpResource` when using the `withFetch` provider. Previously, these options were not exposed. Developers can now include `cache` and `priority` properties directly in the `httpResource` configuration object. The `cache` option accepts values such as `'default'`, `'no-store'`, `'reload'`, `'force-cache'`, and `'only-if-cached'`. The `priority` option accepts `'high'`, `'low'`, and `'auto'` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/55fa38a1e53926d114b2290c084f3540f50b7266, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62301, https://angular.love/angular-20-1-whats-new).

**Affects:** Applications using `httpResource` from `@angular/core/rxjs-interop` with the `withFetch` provider that require control over browser caching or request priority.

**Action:** none: New options are additive and do not require changes to existing code unless the developer wishes to adopt the new functionality.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (20.0):
const data = httpResource(() => ({ url: '/api/data' }));

// After (20.1):
const data = httpResource(() => ({
  url: '/assets/hero.jpg',
  method: 'GET',
  cache: 'force-cache',
  priority: 'high'
}));
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.1.0 adds a new optional `credentials` field to the `HttpResourceRequest` interface in `@angular/common/http`, typed as `RequestCredentials | (string & {})`, documented as "The credentials mode of the request, which determines how cookies and other authentication information are handled. This can affect whether credentials are sent with cross-origin requests or not." The `normalizeRequest` function in `packages/common/http/src/resource.ts` now forwards it into the underlying `HttpRequest` options (`credentials: unwrappedRequest.credentials as RequestCredentials`), so it takes effect when `httpResource` runs on the Fetch backend configured via the `withFetch` feature provider. Previously `httpResource` did not expose the Fetch API's `credentials` option; a separate `withCredentials?: boolean` field already existed on `HttpResourceRequest` and is unchanged (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62390, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/b6ef42843c49e50239b678bb4d8f01ab30589dd3, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.1.0/packages/common/http/src/resource_api.ts)

**Affects:** Additive, opt-in feature. No existing code changes behaviour; only applications that newly pass `credentials` in an `httpResource` request (with the `withFetch` provider) get the new capability.

**Action:** none — no code, configuration, or dependency change required when upgrading to 20.1.0; the new option can simply be adopted where cross-origin cookie/auth/TLS client-certificate control is needed.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// New usage in 20.1.0 (from PR #62390):
httpResource(() => ({
  url: `${ANOTHER_DOMAIN}/api/register`,
  method: 'POST',
  credentials: 'include',
}));

// HttpResourceRequest gains (resource_api.ts @ 20.1.0):
credentials?: RequestCredentials | (string & {});
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.1 added support for the Fetch API's `keepalive` option in requests made via `httpResource` from `@angular/core`, when `HttpClient` is configured with the `withFetch` provider feature (`provideHttpClient(withFetch())`). The `keepalive` flag can be set directly in the reactive request object passed to `httpResource` and is passed through to the native `fetch` call (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61833, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/73269cf5ceb4c32473a81a101a79decd06cfe274). The PR describes it as useful for Beacon API-like functionality (logging analytics on page unload) and better connection management in long-lived apps. The angular.dev `httpResource` guide's request-object example now includes `keepalive: true` alongside options like `mode`, `redirect`, `priority`, `cache`, and `credentials` (https://angular.dev/guide/http/http-resource). A related feature request for exposing `keepalive` is issue #60620.

**Affects:** Applications using `httpResource` with `provideHttpClient(withFetch())` that want (or rely on) `keepalive` behavior; no existing behavior changes for apps that don't set the option — this is purely additive.

**Action:** none — additive feature; adopting `keepalive` in an `httpResource` request object is optional and only has effect when `withFetch` is enabled.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// New usage (Angular 20.1+, requires provideHttpClient(withFetch()))
httpResource(() => ({
  url: '/api/log',
  method: 'POST',
  keepalive: true,
}));
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.1.0 adds `mode` and `redirect` options to `HttpRequestInit` interface and `HttpRequestOptions`, allowing control of CORS and redirect behavior when using `HttpClient` with the Fetch backend. `FetchBackend` was modified to pass these options to the native Fetch API. `mode` supports `'same-origin'`, `'cors'`, `'no-cors'`. `redirect` supports `'follow'`, `'error'`, `'manual'`. These options are available for all HttpClient request methods (`get`, `post`, etc.) and require the fetch backend (default in Angular 20.1.0+). When `withXhr()` is used, a warning message is shown. This enables developers to enforce CORS policies and handle HTTP redirects explicitly. (<https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62315>) (<https://angular.dev/guide/http/making-requests>)

**Affects:** All applications upgrading to Angular 20.1.0. This is a purely additive feature with full backward compatibility. No existing code breaks.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```ts
// POST request with mode and redirect
http.post('/api/register', {}, {
  mode: 'same-origin',
  redirect: 'follow',
}).subscribe();
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.1.0 adds the Fetch API `cache` and `priority` options to `HttpClient`, but only when the request is handled by `FetchBackend`, i.e. `HttpClient` configured via `provideHttpClient(withFetch())` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61766). The PR "Added `cache` and `priority` to the `HttpRequestInit` interface", "Implement[ed] property handling in the `HttpRequest` class" and "Pass[ed] the options through `FetchBackend` to the native Fetch API", while maintaining "full backward compatibility" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61766). On the request object these surface as `HttpRequest.cache` typed `RequestCache` ("Controls how the request will interact with the browser's HTTP cache") and `HttpRequest.priority` typed `RequestPriority` ("Indicates the relative priority of the request"), alongside the existing fetch-only options `credentials` and `keepalive`; the same two members appear in the `HttpRequestOptions` interface used by the `http.get(url, options)` style calls, as `priority?: RequestPriority | undefined` and `cache?: RequestCache | undefined` (https://angular.dev/api/common/http/HttpRequestOptions). Cache values shown in the sources are `'default'`, `'no-store'`, `'reload'`, `'force-cache'`, `'only-if-cached'`; priority values are `'high'`, `'low'`, `'auto'` (). Commit: 9791ab1b6f8694ada6a0e359003243d89d6c7c97 (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9791ab1b6f8694ada6a0e359003243d89d6c7c97).

**Affects:** All applications — purely additive, opt-in per request. Existing code is unaffected unless it already passes `cache`/`priority` in request options, in which case those keys were previously ignored and are now honoured by `FetchBackend`.

**Action:** none (optional: pass `cache`/`priority` in request options when using `provideHttpClient(withFetch())`; note these options have no effect with `HttpXhrBackend`/`withXhr()`).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// New usage in 20.1 with provideHttpClient(withFetch())
// GET request with cache and priority
http.get('/assets/hero-image.jpg', { cache: 'force-cache', priority: 'high' }).subscribe();
```

**What changed:** `httpResource` (from `@angular/common/http`) now supports a `timeout` option on the request object. This new property specifies the timeout for the backend HTTP request in ms (https://angular.dev/api/common/http/HttpResourceRequest). The `HttpResourceRequest` interface was updated to include this option, leveraging the `timeout` capability previously introduced for `HttpClient` in PR #57194 (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62326).

**Affects:** Applications using `httpResource` that require request timeouts.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (or standard request without timeout)
const resource = httpResource(() => ({
  url: '/api/data',
  method: 'GET',
}));

// After (with timeout option)
const resource = httpResource(() => ({
  url: '/api/data',
  method: 'GET',
  timeout: 5000, // Timeout in milliseconds
}));
```

**What changed:** A new optional `timeout` property (type `number | undefined`, milliseconds) was added to `HttpRequestOptions` and accepted by `HttpClient` request methods (`get`, `post`, etc.). The timeout is implemented in both the XHR backend (`HttpXhrBackend`) and the default Fetch backend (`FetchBackend`); when a backend request exceeds the timeout it is aborted and the request fails. Timeouts surface as an `HttpErrorResponse` with a `status` code of `0` whose `error` is a `DOMException` named `TimeoutError`. The timeout applies only to the backend HTTP request itself, not to the whole request-handling chain, so it is not affected by any delay introduced by interceptors (<https://angular.dev/api/common/http/HttpRequestOptions>, <https://angular.dev/guide/http/making-requests>, <https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/57194>, <https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c4cffe2063e790d2f8e4dc8b9c9817f2c4fcc4e7>)
**Affects:** All applications; this is a new opt-in feature. The `timeout` option is inactive unless an application explicitly passes it in the `HttpClient` request options object.
**Action:** none (this change introduces no required code, configuration, dependency, build, or deployment modification; the option is purely additive)
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Set a timeout (in ms) for a request via the options object passed to HttpClient
this.http.get('/api/data', {
  timeout: 3000, // abort the backend request if it takes longer than 3000ms
}).subscribe({
  next: (data) => { /* ... */ },
  error: (err: HttpErrorResponse) => {
    // For timeouts: err.status === 0 and err.error is a DOMException named TimeoutError
    if (err.status === 0 && err.error?.name === 'TimeoutError') {
      console.error('Request timed out');
    }
  },
});
```

**What changed:** The Angular Language Service now implements `getEncodedSemanticClassifications`, returning `ts.Classifications` for templates (both inline and external). Component tags (`TmplAstElement` matched by a component `PotentialDirective`) are classified as the TypeScript `class` token type, enabling semantic syntax highlighting similar to TSX (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60260, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/20c1f991e63b8fc3023a302964d0438bfbfba8f0). In non-`angularOnly` mode, the TS plugin (`ts_plugin.ts`) merges these Angular classifications with those from `tsLS.getEncodedSemanticClassifications` and returns the combined `spans` with the TypeScript service's `endOfLineState` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/20c1f991e63b8fc3023a302964d0438bfbfba8f0). When `format === ts.SemanticClassificationFormat.Original`, the service returns `{spans: [], endOfLineState: ts.EndOfLineState.None}` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/20c1f991e63b8fc3023a302964d0438bfbfba8f0). A new `PerfPhase.LSSemanticClassification` enum value was added to `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/perf/src/api.ts`, with `LAST` moved to the end (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/20c1f991e63b8fc3023a302964d0438bfbfba8f0). The VS Code extension change is tracked as `feat(language-server): Support semantic tokens for components in templates` (https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/main/vscode-ng-language-service/CHANGELOG.md).

**Affects:** Applications using `@angular/language-service` and `@angular/language-server`; the `PerfPhase` enum change affects anything enumerating or hard-coding against `PerfPhase.LAST`.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```ts
